## 3. Exact record-path identity

Fix T finite and a fully specified original record r consisting of k marked events with
\[
 0<t_1<\cdots<t_k<T,\qquad \mu_1,\ldots,\mu_k.
\]
The record includes no further event before T. Put t_0=0, t_(k+1)=T, and define the no-event/jump path operator
\[
 K_f(r)=V_f(T-t_k)J_{\mu_k}V_f(t_k-t_{k-1})\cdots
                       J_{\mu_1}V_f(t_1).                       \tag{4}
\]
For k=0 this means V_f(T). Let P_n be the N-sector projector. A trajectory starting in n is in n+2a during interval (t_a,t_(a+1)). Commuting F only within those fixed sectors in (3) gives the exact identity
\[
 K_f(r)P_n=e^{-i\Phi_r(n)}K_0(r)P_n,\qquad
 \Phi_r(n)=\sum_{a=0}^{k} f(n+2a)(t_{a+1}-t_a).                  \tag{5}
\]
This can equivalently be proved by induction on k, using J_mu P_n=P_(n+2) J_mu. No decomposition of a coherent mark into unobserved sign histories is made.

Because K_0 raises N by 2k, its effect K_0^*K_0 commutes with N. Equation (5) therefore proves the operator identity
\[
 K_f(r)^*K_f(r)=K_0(r)^*K_0(r).                                 \tag{6}
\]
In particular, for EVERY trace-one positive trace-class density rho, including one with number coherences,
\[
 p_f(r;\rho)=\operatorname{Tr}K_f(r)\rho K_f(r)^*
           =p_0(r;\rho)
           =p_0(r;\mathcal E_N\rho),\quad
 \mathcal E_N\rho=\sum_nP_n\rho P_n.                            \tag{7}
\]
The statement is an equality of time densities, not a claim that an exact continuous timestamp has positive point probability.

Bounded Gamma ensures the no-event contraction and jump expansion define the marked path measure. The finite number spectrum and the strictly positive number change also give a stronger termination fact: every nonzero history contains at most floor((n_max-n_min)/2) events, and at most floor((n_max-n)/2) from a specified initial n. Thus the finite-time expansion is a finite sum over mark strings and time simplices. Its positive trace integrals are legitimate for every initial density. Integrating (6) over any measurable record event proves equality for arbitrary timestamp bins, waiting-time laws, ordered or unordered count statistics, events allowing other intervening or later formations, and events involving a censored final waiting time. Equal laws on every finite horizon also give equal cylinder laws over unbounded observation times. No short-time expansion or limit interchange is needed.

