   =e^{-i(\Phi_r(n)-\Phi_r(m))}
      K_0(r)P_n\rho P_m K_0(r)^*.                              \tag{8}
\]
Consequently
\[
 \mathcal E_N\sigma_f(r)=\mathcal E_N\sigma_0(r).                 \tag{9}
\]
The normalization p(r) is the same, so (8)-(9) apply to normalized conditional states wherever p(r)>0, in the usual almost-everywhere sense. For a fully specified classical record, using whichever original instrument was chosen, the two states differ only by a number-diagonal unitary on their output support; its phases generally depend on the event times.

